{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1549745-what-is-the-best-sourcebook-and-rules-set-for-sorcerers-hedge-mages","id":1549745,"title":"What is the best sourcebook and rules' set for sorcerers / hedge mages?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-04-02T11:55:13-07:00","id":1549745,"title":"What is the best sourcebook and rules' set for sorcerers / hedge mages?","content":"Good afternoon, everyone.<br /> <br /> Folks, back in the days of Revised, when I run my Werewolf Chronicles, I made extensive use of the World of Darkness: Sorcerer to portray mortal NPC spellcasters and cultists. Some of most depraved of them even used infernalist paths. I preferred using hedge-mages instead of the ones from Mage: the Ascension because I didn't have access to Mage books, and didn't want to make a crossover.<br /> <br /> Now, I'm about to begin a new Werewolf chronicle, and would love to use sorcerers as NPCs again, as allies and foes.<br /> <br /> As I said, back in the day I used World of Darkness: Sorcerer, which I still have. But I'm aware that there are two other Sorcerer books (Revised and M20) with distinct different systems. Also, I've read a lot of good things about Paths of Power and Sorcerer's Companion (both available at the Storyteller's Vault).<br /> <br /> So, long story short: what would be the best system to use sorcerer NPCs in my Werewolf chronicle? Which one should I get?<br /> <br /> Thanks in advance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28735-greywolf76","name":"Greywolf76","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28735_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-02T12:46:15-07:00","id":1549748,"title":"","content":"There's some matters of taste to consider.<br /> <br /> One of the big advantages the original WoD: Sorcerer has for NPCs, is that the powers are more like Vampire Disciplines with distinct functions at each level or scaling effect per dot. That simplicity can mean a lot for NPCs (esp. relatively squishy NPCs in a game like Werewolf).<br /> <br /> Sorcerer Revised and later (including the STV books) added more complexity to sorcerers. For PCs this is a good thing as it lets players be a bit more flexibly and creative with their magic even if still far more constrained than full on mages. Of course that means if you're the ST doing this for NPCs, that's more stuff for you to mentally juggle while figuring out what your NPCs can do (esp. since the players are going to have their PCs do stuff you didn't entirely expect sometimes).<br /> <br /> So that's the most important distinction for me if we're just talking crossover NPCs.<br /> <br /> If you don't mind the more mechanically complex take will STing them though, I would pick either the official M20 Sorcerer book or Paths of Power. Either is a quality of life improvement over Revised, and the benefits of one vs. the other is much more relevant to playing sorcerer PCs than it is for running sorcerer NPCs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-03T09:52:02-07:00","id":1549778,"title":"","content":"<b>M20 Sorcerer</b> was derived from <b>Paths of Power</b>. It does a few things differently (one of them catastrophically so), but there's a lot of overlap between them. If I had to pick just one, I'd go with <b>Paths of Power</b>; as is, I use it with a handful of tweaks brought over from <b>M20 Sorcerer</b>. In part, the difference is that PoP has a larger page count, and therefore is capable of being more comprehensive.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-07T07:29:39-07:00","id":1550791,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1549745-what-is-the-best-sourcebook-and-rules-set-for-sorcerers-hedge-mages?p=1549748#post1549748\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's some matters of taste to consider.<br /> <br /> One of the big advantages the original WoD: Sorcerer has for NPCs, is that the powers are more like Vampire Disciplines with distinct functions at each level or scaling effect per dot. That simplicity can mean a lot for NPCs (esp. relatively squishy NPCs in a game like Werewolf).<br /> <br /> Sorcerer Revised and later (including the STV books) added more complexity to sorcerers. For PCs this is a good thing as it lets players be a bit more flexibly and creative with their magic even if still far more constrained than full on mages. Of course that means if you're the ST doing this for NPCs, that's more stuff for you to mentally juggle while figuring out what your NPCs can do (esp. since the players are going to have their PCs do stuff you didn't entirely expect sometimes).<br /> <br /> So that's the most important distinction for me if we're just talking crossover NPCs.<br /> <br /> If you don't mind the more mechanically complex take will STing them though, I would pick either the official M20 Sorcerer book or Paths of Power. Either is a quality of life improvement over Revised, and the benefits of one vs. the other is much more relevant to playing sorcerer PCs than it is for running sorcerer NPCs.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1549745-what-is-the-best-sourcebook-and-rules-set-for-sorcerers-hedge-mages?p=1549778#post1549778\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>M20 Sorcerer</b> was derived from <b>Paths of Power</b>. It does a few things differently (one of them catastrophically so), but there's a lot of overlap between them. If I had to pick just one, I'd go with <b>Paths of Power</b>; as is, I use it with a handful of tweaks brought over from <b>M20 Sorcerer</b>. In part, the difference is that PoP has a larger page count, and therefore is capable of being more comprehensive.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you, Heavy Arms and Dataweaver.<br /> <br /> In the end, I've got myself the other two Sorcerer books (Revised and M20), plus Sorcerer's Companion and Paths of Power (the last two are amazing, by the way!). There are a lot of things I liked on those books - and some I really didn't like, particularly how Sorcerer 20th handled aspects. But all of those books gave me enough ideas, optional rules and a solid base to create a single, unified, house-ruled system of linear magic for my campaign for NPCs (and even PCs, if someone ever wants to play as a sorcerer).<br /> <br /> It's far from perfect, sure, but I believe it will work for me (especially because it will be the only spellcasting system I'll be using in the chronicle - no awakened Mages this time around, either).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28735,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28735-greywolf76","user":"Greywolf76","date":". 05-08-2025, 09:08 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28735-greywolf76","name":"Greywolf76","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28735_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-07T22:45:12-07:00","id":1550830,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There are a lot of things I liked on those books - and some I really didn't like, particularly how Sorcerer 20th handled aspects. </div> </div> </div>That was the &quot;\u2026one of them catastrophically so&quot; that I referenced. I even addressed it during the Advanced Copy errata pass\u2026<br /> <br /> I'm glad our advice was helpful.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 05-08-2025, 04:01 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-09T14:18:13-07:00","id":1550892,"title":"","content":"Honestly, one of my biggest problems with M20 Sorcerer was that there were no scrying options for hedge mages. Coupled with the fact that mixing Numina at character creation was locked, it meant that a starting sorcerer could not magically scout out a location at the beginning of the game, and even doing so later on would essentially require ST fiat for them to pick up Clairvoyance of the Psychic Numina.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-10T10:49:49-07:00","id":1550906,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verlan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1549745-what-is-the-best-sourcebook-and-rules-set-for-sorcerers-hedge-mages?p=1550892#post1550892\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly, one of my biggest problems with M20 Sorcerer was that there were no scrying options for hedge mages. Coupled with the fact that mixing Numina at character creation was locked, it meant that a starting sorcerer could not magically scout out a location at the beginning of the game, and even doing so later on would essentially require ST fiat for them to pick up Clairvoyance of the Psychic Numina.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree. That's why although I'm using Revised as the basis for my campaign's sorcerers, I'm taking lots of things from both Sorcerer's Companion and Paths of Power, including Scrying.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28735-greywolf76","name":"Greywolf76","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28735_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-10T11:54:25-07:00","id":1550909,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Greywolf76</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1549745-what-is-the-best-sourcebook-and-rules-set-for-sorcerers-hedge-mages?p=1550906#post1550906\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I agree. That's why although I'm using Revised as the basis for my campaign's sorcerers, I'm taking lots of things from both Sorcerer's Companion and Paths of Power, including Scrying.</div> </div> </div> </div>That sounds like a good idea, luckily there are lots of ways to mix and match the different rulesets.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-06T17:45:08-08:00","id":1556274,"title":"","content":"When I run New World of Darkness or CoD, I like any human, magic-user to be a sorcerer and not a true-mage. It's just that, like Mage: the Ascension, more than Awakening, and I run Requiem and Foresaken, with the only human-mages as hedge-magicians. I do have the OWoD Sorcerers book and I got it so my Mummies (Revised, (Paper-back)), could have hedge-magic. <br /> <br /> I have found, that, Sorcerers are like Vampire-ghouls or human-psychics: they've got one or two powers and they are still mortal.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"738"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-12T03:04:11-08:00","id":1556507,"title":"","content":"Personally, that's where I draw the distinction between &quot;hedge magician&quot; and &quot;sorcerer&quot;: a hedge magician is a dabbler, with one or two powers but still leaning more toward being an ordinary person; this is what you get in <b>Hunters Hunted II</b>, <b>Kinfolk</b>, and <b>Ghosthunters</b>. By contrast, a sorcerer has gone all-in, and his world tends to revolve around sorcery.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-12T16:57:40-08:00","id":1556541,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1549745-what-is-the-best-sourcebook-and-rules-set-for-sorcerers-hedge-mages?p=1556507#post1556507\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally, that's where I draw the distinction between &quot;hedge magician&quot; and &quot;sorcerer&quot;: a hedge magician is a dabbler, with one or two powers but still leaning more toward being an ordinary person; this is what you get in <b>Hunters Hunted II</b>, <b>Kinfolk</b>, and <b>Ghosthunters</b>. By contrast, a sorcerer has gone all-in, and his world tends to revolve around sorcery.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I think this is a good distinction to have in mind, I'm unsure if that's enough for too distinct rules among them.<br /> <br /> Distinct rules still sound to me more of a qualitative/metaphysical distinction than just a quantity/intensity one. Unless the act of &quot;going all-in&quot; significantly changes what a person is, I'm against doing them as two sets of rules, if not strongly so.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-13T17:27:38-08:00","id":1556583,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1549745-what-is-the-best-sourcebook-and-rules-set-for-sorcerers-hedge-mages?p=1556507#post1556507\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally, that's where I draw the distinction between &quot;hedge magician&quot; and &quot;sorcerer&quot;: a hedge magician is a dabbler, with one or two powers but still leaning more toward being an ordinary person; this is what you get in <b>Hunters Hunted II</b>, <b>Kinfolk</b>, and <b>Ghosthunters</b>. By contrast, a sorcerer has gone all-in, and his world tends to revolve around sorcery.</div> </div> </div> </div>Anthropologically, a <i>sorcerer</i>, is someone that's trained in the arts of magic; a <i>witch</i> (Hedge-Magician), using sheer willpower (that's the textbook-definition), to work their magic. And yeah, I can totally see a Hunters Hunted character, being a Hedge, known only one Numinia whereas a sorcerer, from the book, may know other spells.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"738"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-14T01:45:09-08:00","id":1556597,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1549745-what-is-the-best-sourcebook-and-rules-set-for-sorcerers-hedge-mages?p=1556541#post1556541\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> While I think this is a good distinction to have in mind, I'm unsure if that's enough for too distinct rules among them.<br /> <br /> Distinct rules still sound to me more of a qualitative/metaphysical distinction than just a quantity/intensity one. Unless the act of &quot;going all-in&quot; significantly changes what a person is, I'm against doing them as two sets of rules, if not strongly so.</div> </div> </div> </div>They're not two rulesets, per se; but Sorcerers _do_ use a generalization of the same rules that dabblers use. Dabblers (HH2, KF, and GH) have a specific selection of Attribute+Ability as their dice pool; they don't get to select it based off of a Focus, as is the case in <b>M20 Sorcerer</b> and <b>Sorcerer: Paths of Power</b>. Even earlier editions of <b>Sorcerer</b> have each sorcerer using their own instruments to perform magic, something that's either glossed over or standardized for the dabblers.<br /> <br /> As well, the dabbler versions of Paths don't have Aspects; everything's built into a linear progression of how many successes you achieve, with no ability to customize the effect of a casting by choosing how the successes are applied. While that's also true of the first edition of <b>Sorcerer</b>, all subsequent editions have used Aspects, whereas none of the &quot;dabbler Paths&quot; <i>ever </i>have, no matter <i>when </i>they were published.<br /> <br /> In short, the dabbler Paths are simplified versions of the sorcerous Paths: more rigid and inflexible, but easier to learn and use. <br /> <hr /><br /> As well, the original <b>World of Darkness: Sorcerer</b> &quot;significantly change what a person is&quot; when they went all-in. Some excerpts:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">At the basis of any sorcerer\u2019s call to magic is the Yearning \u2014 the inexorable feeling that something, somewhere, is calling to the magus. That \u201csomething\u201d is the Otherworld, and the Yearning is the innate human desire to connect with something greater than our human senses and abilities normally perceive.<br /> Those who seek the magician\u2019s road see glimmers of the Otherworld in many things: philosophy, poetry, the arts. The average initiate of the Mysteries explores all of these avenues and others. (Psychoactive drugs, religious meditations, alternative lifestyles and self-mortification are common \u201csymptoms\u201d of a greater quest.) All of these avenues are explored and examined, but ultimately fail to quench the Yearning. Glimmers of the Otherworld are not enough; the potential magician hungers for something more intense \u2014 something imagined, never truly felt, but innately understood.<br /> <br /> The Otherworld is universal, even omniversal, but the sorcerer tends to see it through the lenses of his culture and beliefs. The Otherworld perceived by the Nephite Priesthood (who set their roots in 19th-century Mormonism) is the same Otherworld perceived by the Fenian of Ireland and the Mesoamerican Balamob shamans. Each group perceives it differently, however, and each one clings to the idea that his vision is the only true one.<br /> <br /> Thus, all sorcerers are drawn to the Otherworld, to different roads leading to the same destination. That road itself is vital to the sorcerer who hopes to survive his initiation. Each road might cross different terrain, but it still provides the traveler some measure of safety. Straying from the road is dangerous. Remember the fairy tales that instruct you never to step off the path? That warning rings especially true to the followers of the mystic arts.</div> <br /> (WoD:S pp.11\u201312)<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Thus, the first step along the path is often the last. Magically inclined searchers satisfy their Yearnings through any number of means, spurious or genuine. Few of them truly reach the Otherworld they seek; they might brush against it now and then, but fail to grasp the prize when they find it. Fewer still recognize the Yearning for what it is: a genuine numinous experience, a spiritual revelation.<br /> <br /> Few are lucky enough to find the true path, obscured among the bramble and thorns of modern esotericism. There, a searcher finds some font of truth and immerses himself in its cold, mysterious water.</div> <br /> (WoD:S p.14)<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Where the hungry yearns for the Otherworld, and the student learns its ways, the adept now interacts with it. But just like the student, the adept still has much to discover. Straddling this world and the Otherworld, the adept demonstrates the ability to move the world around him. He has learned the language of the spirits, and understands how to look into the future. The elements are his to command, and the dreamscape opens before him.</div> <br /> (WoD:S p.15)<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">The Black Art is a one-way journey, too. Stepping on the road to sorcery cannot be undone. Learning the arts of magic is not like going to a vocational school. The student cannot simply drop out and forget he ever tried. You see, magical opening is a two-way process: Just as you learn more about the universe, the universe is learning more about you. You have announced yourself to the spirits, to the angels, to the demons \u2014 to whatever concept your path is teaching you.<br /> <br /> Once a magician has set out to find the darkness (or the light, if he can), there can be no running away, no forgetting or ignoring, no money-back guarantees. He has changed his perceptions forever.<br /> <br /> This presents one of the paradoxes of magic. You can play with magic your whole life and never truly step on the path. And you can just as easily tread the path without realizing it.<br /> <br /> In many ways, the Black Art is a destiny, not a choice. You don\u2019t choose magic \u2014 it chooses you.</div> <br /> (WoD:S p.16)<br /> <br /> It's very subtle, and can be mistaken for merely poetic language. But taken literally, this describes a metaphysical distinction between sorcerers (who have a Yearning for the Otherworld that their practice of the sorcerous Paths fulfills) and dabblers (who lack that Yearning). That second-to-last paragraph quoted from p.16 in particular points out a distinction between those who &quot;play with magic&quot; and those who are &quot;on the path&quot;, though at the same time saying that it's easy to mistake one for the other.<br /> <br /> Anyone can dabble in the Paths; but only someone chosen by magic itself can become a sorcerer.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 12-14-2025, 04:58 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-14T20:55:51-08:00","id":1556632,"title":"","content":"I personally prefer the Sorcerer M20 version. It\u2019s really well written and I feel like it really captures the essence of the various sorcerer groups. If I had a player who wanted to play a sorcerer, I would direct them to that book.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-15T01:34:56-08:00","id":1556635,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iguazu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1549745-what-is-the-best-sourcebook-and-rules-set-for-sorcerers-hedge-mages?p=1556583#post1556583\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Anthropologically, a <i>sorcerer</i>, is someone that's trained in the arts of magic; a <i>witch</i> (Hedge-Magician), using sheer willpower (that's the textbook-definition), to work their magic. And yeah, I can totally see a Hunters Hunted character, being a Hedge, known only one Numinia whereas a sorcerer, from the book, may know other spells.</div> </div> </div> </div>Can you cite your sources on this?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1549745-what-is-the-best-sourcebook-and-rules-set-for-sorcerers-hedge-mages?p=1556597#post1556597\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's very subtle, and can be mistaken for merely poetic language. But taken literally, this describes a metaphysical distinction between sorcerers (who have a Yearning for the Otherworld that their practice of the sorcerous Paths fulfills) and dabblers (who lack that Yearning). That second-to-last paragraph quoted from p.16 in particular points out a distinction between those who &quot;play with magic&quot; and those who are &quot;on the path&quot;, though at the same time saying that it's easy to mistake one for the other.<br /> <br /> Anyone can dabble in the Paths; but only someone chosen by magic itself can become a sorcerer.</div> </div> </div> </div>My problem here (and here specifically) is that it doesn't really base this distinction on dedication.<br /> <br /> It's either impossible to learn magic without the Yearning (which would make normal humans incapable of learning magic no matter what), or anyone lacking the Yearning is incapable of advance, being locked into a dabbler, again no matter what. Even then, the Yearning is no guarantee of becoming a sorcerer, which means going all in or not isn't really what makes you into a Sorcerer. You do have your life changed by becoming a Sorcerer, but that doesn't really implies you're &quot;going all in&quot; more than the Otherworld will be a permanent fixture in your life from now on.<br /> <br /> So this really isn't a distinction between a dabbler and a &quot;professional&quot;, despite using the word, it's the definition of a new splat. Which isn't by itself a problem at all, but is another thing entirely.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-15T12:16:10-08:00","id":1556660,"title":"","content":"Fair enough, with one caveat: the Yearning means that every sorcerer sought it out. What you're pointing out is the possibility of a dabbler who's as dedicated to studying magic as sorcerers are. In game terms, this would be a character whose concept is &quot;a dedicated student of magic,&quot; but is built using HH2/KF/GH Paths rather than Sorcerer Paths. <br /> <br /> But the reason I ended up using the terminology I did is because the descriptions given in HH2/KF/GH are of people who aren't <i>primarily </i>interested in the magic; they're only interested in it as a tool for hunting (in the case of HH2 and GH or participating in the world of their shapeshifting kin (in the case of KF). There may be exceptions to this; but they <i>are </i>exceptions. And as a result, the Paths in those books are described as something cobbled together by people stumbling around in the dark. <br /> <br /> By contrast, every edition of Sorcerer describes them as members of societies dedicated to the study and use of magic, and the Paths are something you learn from teachers who are already well-versed in them. Self-taught sorcerers are vanishingly rare.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-15T19:26:04-08:00","id":1556690,"title":"","content":"I partially agree, but I want to bring to attention one passage:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1549745-what-is-the-best-sourcebook-and-rules-set-for-sorcerers-hedge-mages?p=1556597#post1556597\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">(WoD:S p.15)<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">The Black Art is a one-way journey, too. Stepping on the road to sorcery cannot be undone. Learning the arts of magic is not like going to a vocational school. The student cannot simply drop out and forget he ever tried. You see, magical opening is a two-way process: Just as you learn more about the universe, the universe is learning more about you. You have announced yourself to the spirits, to the angels, to the demons \u2014 to whatever concept your path is teaching you.<br /> <br /> Once a magician has set out to find the darkness (or the light, if he can), there can be no running away, no forgetting or ignoring, no money-back guarantees. He has changed his perceptions forever.<br /> <br /> This presents one of the paradoxes of magic. You can play with magic your whole life and never truly step on the path. And you can just as easily tread the path without realizing it.<br /> <br /> In many ways, the Black Art is a destiny, not a choice. You don\u2019t choose magic \u2014 it chooses you.</div></div> </div> </div> </div>This one makes clear that the character doesn't have to <i>currently</i> be that invested. They don't have a choice anymore, but that's less about dedication, continued drive or even pressure to have magic as your main calling in life, and more the pragmatic need to deal with supernatural entities becoming a fixture of your life. So, a character that &quot;stumbled&quot; on the Path soon in their life and dedicate most of their time and efforts to other endeavors would still be a Sorcerer, but not a dedicated student.<br /> <br /> I mean, sure, as presented it does indicate <i>most</i> of those people will be exactly like you said. But not necessarily so, not as a consequence of being a dabbler or a dedicated student. And the books don't do much to further link one to the other, which could be done. It could be the case, for example, that dedication is what <i>causes</i> the Yearning. Or that without it magic causes disinterest through a <i>Mists</i>-like effect as is common through the WoD, so that hunters can force themselves to learn <i>something</i>, but only the Yearning lets you keep focus and interest. It could be that the organizations Sorcerers form are what keep it like that, both denying development to those who didn't reach the Otherworld and coercing those who did to keep at it regardless.<br /> <br /> Heck, even the followers of Mages, which can hardly be deemed dabblers, end up using either Hedge-Magic rules or at best a couple simplified rotes, falling way behind Sorcerers.<br /> <br /> A lot could be done to make this about the difference between ignorant dabblers and informed dedicated students, but as is I feel they <i>tried</i>, but it isn't really that. Instead what we got is a new splat of magic-users that's exactly that, a new splat, if a minor one. I don't personally see it as a relevant addition to the WoD, regardless of how their rules are similar or distinct of others, or how good they are (I'm not necessarily against using their rules for every human magic-user).<br /> <br /> My personal headcanon is that all those are just different sets of rules to represent the same general phenomenon, and you chose which one fits best the current game.<br /> <br /> But all that said, I'm not dead-set on dismissing Sorcerers as a splat. Being unconvinced that they fill the role of human magic-users doesn't mean I'm not open to arguments on what make them an interesting splat on their own. I don't know them enough to already know many of those arguments, so I'm even curious.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-18T15:57:47-08:00","id":1556811,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1549745-what-is-the-best-sourcebook-and-rules-set-for-sorcerers-hedge-mages?p=1556635#post1556635\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Can you cite your sources on this?<br /> . . ..</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know: I read too many books; try these:<br /> <br /> <i>Cultural Anthropology</i> by Frank Robert Vivelo<br /> C<i>ultural Anthropology 7th Edition</i> by Nanda and Warms<br /> <i>Cultural Sketches 6th Edition</i> by Holly Peeters-Golden<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"738"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-18T17:17:06-08:00","id":1556813,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1549745-what-is-the-best-sourcebook-and-rules-set-for-sorcerers-hedge-mages?p=1556635#post1556635\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Can you cite your sources on this?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>It's pretty commonplace. It is confusing to Westerners because it violates our categories. <br /> <br /> Anthropologists will also say plausible level of power Sorcerer. Impossible powers witch.<br /> <br /> Uses secret poisons to kill at a distance, Sorcerer. <br /> <br /> Their soul leaves their body at night turns in a wild beast and hunts people, Witch.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-18T19:15:03-08:00","id":1556819,"title":"","content":"It's a somewhat weird claim to say that anthropology, a largely Western based field of study, violates Western categorizations.<br /> <br /> Also... since I'm not paying to get a book for a thread on the Internet, what I can find that's freely available basically amounts to there not being a scholarly consensus on where to draw the line between a &quot;sorcerer&quot; and a &quot;witch,&quot; or even if such a distinction is academically valid. I can find a lot more university's with open source journals having articles pointing out that definitions from the 70s don't really pan out as far too many cultures do not follow these clean cut ideas of &quot;sorcerers do magic this way, and witches do magic that way,&quot; for the terms to be meaningful as a direction of study. Not trying to fit traditional magical practices into Western built boxes and instead learn what people mean from their cultural concepts of magical expressions is actually what baseline anthropology is.<br /> <br /> I've also found far more references to the idea that the distinction is meant to be &quot;innate&quot; vs. &quot;learned&quot; magic, not &quot;sheer willpower,&quot; vs. ritualized practice as originally stated in the thread. I couldn't find anyone talking about will-working outside of talking about more recent Western neo-occultists derived from folks like John Dee. If any distinction in the WoD books comes close to this, it's Psychic Numina vs Sorcerous Numina. The idea that a witch might not even be aware of their impact on others vs. a sorcerer that is always intentional that some anthropologists seem to be getting at, follows that mechanical distinction far more than the word &quot;hedge&quot; applied to things does.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-20T04:00:16-08:00","id":1556852,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1549745-what-is-the-best-sourcebook-and-rules-set-for-sorcerers-hedge-mages?p=1556819#post1556819\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's a somewhat weird claim to say that anthropology, a largely Western based field of study, violates Western categorizations.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Not in the least. I was talking about ordinary on the street categories. Take the idea of &quot;Conspicuous Consumption&quot; the idea that extravagant and pointlessly wasteful spending to enhance an individual's social standing. The idea that something deliberately stupid and burdensome, and known to be so, can enhance your social standing and grant an aura of authority and value is bizarre. But an examination of the world around us shows us that it works. Many historical societies, including colonial America, often tried to limit who could participate in Conspicuous Consumption in order to protect the social hierarchy. <br /> <br /> The anthropological idea of &quot;witch&quot; isn't based on Western cultural norms. Whatever you think of anthropology's historical track record the early anthropologists wanted a universal definition of witch. What they came up with uses the commonplace word 'witch' to describe something that isn't much if anything like our day to day ordinary concepts of the meaning of the word 'witch.' <br /> <br /> Weird it may be, but it is also what they did.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-20T05:13:37-08:00","id":1556854,"title":"","content":"I don't find anything weird about &quot;conspicuous consumption.&quot; Anthropologists and psychologists have well established how credibility enhancing displays function as social currency by creating the appearance (real or not) of conviction in one's beliefs since only someone that really believes in something would give up valuable items for it; which is something humans tend to value as social organisms. That even applies to the wealthy (or those caught up in trying to fake it) flaunting their wealth and then reinforcing the social hierarchy that puts the rich on the top of the ladder. These constructs extend to many other animals in the form of mating behaviors that fall under the good gene hypothesis from biology. There's a deference between what feels intuitively logical on the scale of individuals, and what actually works in larger social structures and thus gets replicated and prioritized.<br /> <br /> Some anthropologists trying to generate a universal definition of &quot;witch&quot; isn't weird because it doesn't abide by common Western conceptualizations of witches (which isn't even much of thing since that has plenty of its own variations, making it weird to assume there is one universal Western &quot;witch&quot; category). It's a long challenged concept because it doesn't accurately map to anything in the data. A universal definition that doesn't actually end up describing what more than a small percentage of what people mean within their cultural contexts is a useless taxonomy in anthropology.<br /> <br /> The fact that it's hard to remove outdated concepts from text books is an entirely separate issue in academia; if somewhat relevant when we're told to read a text book first published in the 70s by a scholar that stayed in the field for almost three decades after that (including new editions of said text) and was strongly incentivized by the system to not overthrow his own positions despite significant push back from others in the field.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-21T16:43:19-08:00","id":1556883,"title":"","content":"More to the point, there's no need to use it that way here. The vampire hunters who have picked up a HH2 Path aren't witches; they're vampire hunters who have dabbled in magic. &quot;Dabblers&quot; catches this meaning very effectively <i>without </i>invoking a potentially misleading term.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-21T20:23:31-08:00","id":1556891,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure it's a very effective avoidance of misleading labels even if it's just in a different category. When does someone stop being a dabbler? How many dots in Numina Paths does a HH2 character, or a Kinfolk, etc. have to grab before they're not a dabbler? Can I have a Kinfolk with 20+ dots spread over multiple Numina Paths that's still only a &quot;dabbler&quot; to a Sorcerer with 4 dots in one Path?<br /> <br /> The problem is that dabbler is a subjective term that doesn't discriminate in-kind of what's being done. Someone that dabbles in FPS video games is not doing something different than a FPS esports pro; just invested to a much lesser degree. The whole issue with trying to have Dabblers vs. real Sorcerers with Yearnings is that it tries to create a metaphysical difference instead of an investment difference that the word dabbler implies. There's no clear reason that a vampire hunter and a sorcerer are such separate categories that being a vampire hunter means you can never do more than dabble in magic Paths, or that a Sorcerer can't be motivated by a desire to destroy vampires with their magic and would only dabble in such incidentally (as I tend to bang on frequently when it comes to the HH books: being a hunter in those books has never been presented as a splat exclusive thing, the HH series as a hunter group that's made up <u>of vampires</u>).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-21T21:41:00-08:00","id":1556893,"title":"","content":"I'm not saying that there isn't a gray area; there absolutely is. And if you want to treat them as a spectrum in your Chronicles, more power to you. All I'm saying is that if you want to treat sorcerers as a separate thing from the HH2/KF/GH Path users, there's precedent for it. <br /> <br /> And given that the presentation of hunters and kinfolk with Paths leans in to the &quot;isolated people who don't really know what they're dealing with&quot; vibe while sorcerers are presented as only rarely <i>not </i>being members of societies which pass down secret knowledge to their members, calling the former &quot;dabblers&quot; and the latter &quot;sorcerers&quot; will be right more often than not.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-22T01:03:38-08:00","id":1556895,"title":"","content":"You're arguing for your own preferences. Nobody is telling you how to play, you're just getting push back because you're persistently presenting material that focuses on your specific desires. The game has presented a full spectrum of how to handle Numina. There is no choice but to run it based on what you want to use to suit your tastes because if you tried to include 2e, Rev. and 20th fully and equally you'd have an incoherent mess. You're the one that brought up the whole &quot;I want a distinction&quot; thing, which spun out the external definition tangent. It's the people that don't want that, that should be hearing that what you're saying is one way to handle it per the books; not the way the books actually solely advocate for.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-22T22:28:48-08:00","id":1556930,"title":"","content":"I never claimed otherwise. The only thing I said was that me preferences aren't unsupported.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-22T23:47:30-08:00","id":1556932,"title":"","content":"Yeah, technically, you might not have claimed otherwise. That doesn't change that you felt like debating monteparnas about it despite her simply stating what her preferences were and why your quotes weren't convincing her to change them, nor does it change that you switched from the first person to the second person in your response to me (which shifts the tone from discussing your personal desires to a broader category of claim).<br /> <br /> And it certainly isn't like you're engaging with my point that trying to get the conversation back to &quot;dabblers vs sorcerers&quot; instead of &quot;witches vs sorcerers,&quot; isn't really about avoiding potentially misleading terms. All you had to say was we should try to get back to the game terms instead of anthropology terms to refocus on games instead of going down an academic tangential rabbit hole.<br /> <br /> Or, to be characteristically blunt: it really feels like you want to claim otherwise and are dancing up to the line but know you shouldn't cross it, rather than you're not claiming otherwise because you disagree with the position entailed. You have said significantly more than there is textual support for your preferences (such are reading WoD:S a poetic instead of literal is a mistake; an uncessary point to make if you simple want to establish there is a reason within the text that guides your preferences), and nobody has tried to say there isn't.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-25T22:06:44-08:00","id":1557003,"title":"","content":"I didn't came here earlier because I was sick and seriously lacking energy, barely leaving my room, and the computer from where I use the forums isn't there.<br /> <br /> Heavy Arms said almost everything I wanted to, but I'll add a few points here that I learned when I had Anthropology courses in the University.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1549745-what-is-the-best-sourcebook-and-rules-set-for-sorcerers-hedge-mages?p=1556852#post1556852\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The anthropological idea of &quot;witch&quot; isn't based on Western cultural norms. Whatever you think of anthropology's historical track record the early anthropologists wanted a universal definition of witch. What they came up with uses the commonplace word 'witch' to describe something that isn't much if anything like our day to day ordinary concepts of the meaning of the word 'witch.'</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually it is <b><i>completely</i></b> based on Western cultural norms, and that's the problem.<br /> <br /> It doesn't &quot;violates&quot; ordinary categories more than any other term turned into technical parlance in academy. Anthropologists have a history of using terms like Sorcerer, Witch and Shaman precisely because they wanted to derive a universal theory of social roles where those things existed in every culture, just under other names.<br /> <br /> But while this practice hasn't completely faded, it faces ever more criticism because it has no confirmation on the actual data. On top of the proponents of those categories being unable to agree on key aspects of their definitions, and their categorization being completely arbitrary instead of really based on any consistent use of the terms, the fact is that their categories fail to add any meaningful information to the actual observations. They don't survive even a decent exploration of the ways Europeans used those very terms in the last 15 centuries or so.<br /> <br /> Other than that, they're <i><b>exactly</b></i> European cultural norms (that aren't even that normative in Europe) being extended to everyone else.<br /> <br /> Conspicuous Consumption is a well attested phenomena and no culture overtly recognizes it as such as they interpret it under cultural lenses, yet it exists everywhere, including modern &quot;Western&quot; culture. Rich people do that all the fucking time in every country, and most people below them try to get their shot at it as well as they can.<br /> <br /> Not to say the very concept of &quot;Western world/culture/whatever&quot; is far from well established or accepted, with too many critics and defenders, and a lot of dissenting on how exactly it is defined.\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-26T00:11:32-08:00","id":1557004,"title":"","content":"Ah, a discussion about witches and what they are, is it?<br /> <br /> (Looks down to PoD copy of Fangs at the Gate).<br /> <br /> Vaguely defined? No real structure? A complete lack of qualifiers and concrete themes? A word that doesn't really agree on the same things when two or more different people use it?<br /> <br /> Sounds about right.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude02","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-26T07:00:20-08:00","id":1557008,"title":"","content":"First, there really needs to be a cross game index of all Sorcery Paths, Numina, Psi powers, etc. I think there's at least 40 in that list. Many overlap to various degrees. <br /> <br /> Second, you can always use a trick from the early Vampire and Werewolf books. They simply had Mages use the same powers. They said they weren't the same, but could use the same stats as a temporary stopgap. <br /> <br /> That trick was ditched with the first Mage book but it isn't useless now. Simply allow your campaign's sorcerers access to the same powers as the other supernatural beings in your campaign with fewer restrictions and lower cost. Sorcerers will still be weaker but they'll have a wider arsenal of powers and more flexibility. And you get your desired simplification. <br /> <br /> Myself, I love sphere magic!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-26T13:47:27-08:00","id":1557012,"title":"","content":"A index is useful to some of us (and I'm pretty sure there's a least two fan wikis out there with their attempts at it), but it's not really something the game itself would have cared about doing. One of the reasons for the overlap is because each game line wanted to have self-contained &quot;human&quot; magic that fit the goals of their specific designs.<br /> <br /> Also, that &quot;trick&quot; wasn't really ditched. Mage didn't tend to use it because Spheres are too open ended to replicate specific monsters with specific powers. Changeling and Wraith both used the same trick, as did pretty much all the future books (including the 20th edition ones, and the CofD) outside of Mage. It's much easier to say &quot;replicate a lupine's monster form with Potence&quot; or &quot;replicate a leech's blood fueled speed with some Rage,&quot; than it is to try to give them enough juice behind a Time 3 multiaction effect without implying way more power than you mean to.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-26T22:24:59-08:00","id":1557036,"title":"","content":"Right. What would be needed for <i>that </i>would be something like <b>Mage: the Awakening</b>\u200b's Proximi, where they have a set of Blessings that are each mechanically equivalent to a Rote. Interestingly, take a look at the Hedge Magician from <b>Gods and Monsters</b>, pp.53\u201354.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}